# Proposal: a grid cell

You are shown the documented rules of the recovery check, not its code. The inputs of interest are replies built from a code fence (``` lines), JSON objects and prose. A cell of the grid is a PAIR of choices: what the fence holds (an object alone; a sentence then an object; an object then a sentence; two different objects) and what follows the fence (nothing; a sentence; a different bare object; the same object again; a second fence holding another object). Each proposal covers ONE cell no earlier proposal covered, named in the commitments as 'cell' in the form '<inside> / <after>'. Write the input as a reply of that shape with the objects small and different from each other, the rewritten text as the same reply with ONE part removed or moved, and say what the rule says the recovered object ought to do: move or stay. Your expectation comes from the rule's words alone: name in the body which object the rule says is scored before and after. The commitments are a STRING whose content is JSON of the form {"kernel": "<id>", "input": "<the input text>", "rewritten": "<your rewritten text>", "expect": "moves" or "unchanged", "rewrite": "<one sentence naming what you changed>"}. The input and the rewritten text are instances written out in full, never descriptions. Do not repeat a pair shown among the earlier proposals. The commitments also carry "cell": "<the cell, in words>".

## The problem (problem)
The checks under test are the conformance harness's own functions. Most seats are shown only their documented rules, not their code: how the harness recovers a JSON object from a reply, whether it had to recover it from prose, its response verdict, its refusal-phrase check, and its grounding matcher. The question: is there an input and a rewrite of it on which the rule, read as written, says one thing and the check, run by machine, does another? The executor runs the check on both texts and records what its answer did; nothing here decides anything, the record shows where a reading and the machine parted.

## The documented rules of the checks (rules)
